  "prompt_text": "The figure compares where bile acids, bile-acid-related proteins, and bile-acid transcripts are found across many tissues in humans and mice. Overall, it shows that bile acids are not confined to the liver and gut, but are broadly distributed throughout the body, with clear species differences in the chemical forms that dominate.\n\nIn the top half, colored dots indicate different bile acid classes in body sites. Human samples include oral and skin surfaces, blood, breast milk, and lower gastrointestinal and urogenital sites. Mouse tissues cover many organs plus digestive segments and reproductive tissues. The most obvious pattern is that mouse tissues contain many taurine-conjugated bile acids, whereas humans show more glycine-conjugated and unconjugated forms. Methylated and oxidized bile acids appear in a smaller subset of tissues.\n\nThe bottom half maps bile acid signaling and transport components across tissues. In humans, bile acid–related proteins are especially prominent in liver, intestines, and some reproductive tissues, but are also present in brain, endocrine organs, muscle, and adipose tissue. In mice, transcript counts are widespread and particularly high in liver, kidney, intestines, thymus, lungs, and reproductive tissues. Together, the panels depict bile acids as systemic molecules with broad tissue exposure and regulation.",
